---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What should I learn from CELPIP sample answers?",
    answer:
      "Study the structure, tone, and idea development. Those are the parts that transfer well to your own writing and speaking.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I memorize CELPIP sample answers?",
    answer:
      "No. Memorizing full answers usually makes your language sound stiff, repetitive, and less natural under pressure.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I use sample answers more effectively?",
    answer:
      "Read one model, identify what makes it work, then write or speak your own version on a similar prompt while keeping the same structure.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "CELPIP Sample Answers", path: "/blog/celpip-sample-answers/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="CELPIP Sample Answers | Study Models Without Sounding Memorized"
  description="A practical guide to using CELPIP sample answers for writing and speaking without copying, memorizing, or repeating weak patterns."
  type="article"
  keywords="CELPIP sample answers, CELPIP writing samples, CELPIP speaking sample answers"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag">CELPIP study guide</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 0;">CELPIP sample answers</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        Sample answers are useful when you study them like a teacher, not like a memorizer.
        The goal is to notice why the response works, then build your own answer with the
        same control.
      </p>
      <p>
        Students improve faster when they copy the structure and not the exact sentences.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What to copy</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The order of ideas.</li>
          <li>The balance between main point and support.</li>
          <li>The tone used for the task.</li>
          <li>The way the answer starts and finishes clearly.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What not to copy</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>Whole paragraphs or speeches word for word.</li>
          <li>Vocabulary you cannot use naturally.</li>
          <li>Examples that do not fit your own prompt.</li>
          <li>Mistakes hidden inside a weak sample.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>A better way to study one sample</h2>
      <div class="guide-steps">
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Read for structure</h3>
            <p>Find the opening, the support, and the closing before you focus on vocabulary.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Mark useful language</h3>
            <p>Keep only the phrases that are clear, natural, and easy for you to reuse.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Close the model</h3>
            <p>Do not keep looking at it while you create your own response.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Compare after</h3>
            <p>Check whether your answer is just as clear and complete, not whether it is identical.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>The biggest trap</h2>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          The biggest trap is false confidence. A model answer can look easy when you read
          it slowly, but real improvement only happens when you produce your own answer
          under time pressure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/celpip/writing/samples">
          <strong>Open CELPIP writing samples</strong>
          <span>Study sample essays with a clearer purpose.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/blog/celpip-writing-task-1-email">
          <strong>Read the Task 1 email guide</strong>
          <span>See how sample structure works inside a real writing task.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/celpip/writing/ai-feedback">
          <strong>Try the writing score lab</strong>
          <span>Get scoring and lesson suggestions on your own response.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
